title: Microsoft Defender BTR.sys Driver Abuse for EDR Bypass
id: c9e5a3f6-0d4b-4e8f-b7a2-3f9c6d0e4b8a
status: experimental
description: >
    Detects suspicious deployment of BTR.sys, the Microsoft Defender Boot-Time
    Removal driver, outside normal Defender remediation flows. Check Point Research
    and Jiri Vinopal (Aug 2026) disclosed how this legitimate Microsoft-signed
    driver can be weaponized to perform kernel-level file and registry operations
    before EDR products initialize, enabling arbitrary file deletion and defense
    evasion.
references:
    - https://cyberpress.org/microsoft-signed-defender-driver-weaponized/
    - https://gbhackers.com/windows-defender-driver-abuse-enables-edr-bypass/
author: Tia N. List
date: 2026/08/21
tags:
    - attack.t1089
    - attack.t1562
logsource:
    category: process_creation
    product: windows
detection:
    selection_driver:
        Image|endswith:
            - '\BTR.sys'
            - '\btr.sys'
    selection_resource:
        Image|endswith: '\MpEngine.dll'
        CommandLine|contains: 'BOOTTIMETOOL'
    condition: selection_driver or selection_resource
falsepositives:
    - Legitimate Microsoft Defender remediation deploying BTR.sys during scheduled scans
    - Defender maintenance operations extracting the BOOTTIMETOOL resource
level: high
